course: dictionary
lesson: 2193
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The T-shirt is made of soft cotton.» You wear a T-shirt, so cotton here is the cloth.
explain: Cotton can also be the soft white fibre that grows around the seeds of a plant. In «Cotton grows around the seeds», the word grows is the clue: this is the plant's fibre, not a cloth.
explain: Cotton is also part of other words, such as «cotton wool» and «cotton buds», where it names a soft pad or stick. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Cotton is a natural material from a plant, unlike nylon or polyester, which are made by people.
fact: «Cotton» can mean the soft fibre, the cloth or thread made from it, or soft fluffy pads made from it.
fact: Cotton is soft, absorbent and breathable, which is why it is good for T-shirts and towels.
fact: «Cotton wool» (UK) and «cotton balls» (USA) are the same soft pads, used for cleaning skin or cuts.
fact: «Cotton candy» (USA) is called candyfloss in the UK and fairy floss in Australia.
exercise: In «The T-shirt is made of soft cotton», what is cotton? | A metal used for making pans | *Cloth made from the soft fibre | A type of bird | A kind of sweet
exercise: «Cotton grows around the seeds of the plant.» What does cotton grow around? | Leaves | Stones | Water | *Seeds
exercise: Which of these is a natural material, not made by people? | Plastic | Polyester | *Cotton | Nylon
test: Doctors say not to push anything into your ear. Which can damage the eardrum? | Warm baths | Clean socks | *Cotton buds pushed deep into the ear | Gentle hugs
test: In the USA, «cotton candy» is called what in the UK? | Cotton balls | Cotton wool | Fairy floss | *Candyfloss
exam: Which word means the same as UK «cotton wool»? | Cotton T-shirt | *Cotton balls | Cotton candy | Cotton plant
